import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

/*
We are given a permutation p[1..N].
Operation:
- choose a subarray and cyclically shift it left by 1, cost A
- choose a subarray and cyclically shift it right by 1, cost B

We need minimum cost to sort into [1..N].

Key observation:
A left shift on subarray [l..r] moves p[l] to position r and shifts p[l+1..r] left.
A right shift on subarray [l..r] moves p[r] to position l and shifts p[l..r-1] right.

Build the sorted array from left to right.

Suppose we want to place value i at position i.
Let current position of i be pos >= i.

Two ways:
1) Use one right shift on subarray [i..pos], cost B.
   This places i directly at position i.
   Elements in [i..pos-1] shift right by 1.

2) Equivalent effect can also be achieved by repeatedly using left shifts involving earlier chosen boundaries.
   Globally, after processing prefix, the total left-shift contribution depends only on how many elements are "pulled from the front" of unresolved positions.
   This leads to a DP on inversion count style:
   For each element i in increasing order of value, let x_i be how many larger elements before i in the original permutation.
   Then placing i using right shift costs B, while leaving it to be handled by left-shift structure costs A per crossed element.
   Minimum becomes sum over inversions with weight min(A,B)?? Not enough.

A cleaner invariant:
Any operation changes inversion count by exactly +/- (len-1) with sign depending on whether endpoint element moves across others.
To sort a permutation, each inversion pair must be corrected by some crossing.
A left shift can move first element right across k elements, fixing k inversions where that element was too small/large accordingly.
A right shift can move last element left across k elements.

For a permutation, minimum cost equals:
sum over cycles of permutation mapping position -> value:
For each cycle, if its length is L, we need to rotate elements into place.
But arbitrary subarray cyclic shifts allow resolving a cycle by repeatedly extracting the needed element from its current position to the target position.
Each such extraction over distance d costs either A or B independent of d, which means we want to maximize moved distance per operation.

Standard DP:
Let f[i] = min cost to sort prefix 1..i after removing values 1..i into place.
When placing i+1, if its current position among remaining elements is k (1-indexed), then:
- bring it to front of remaining by right shift cost B
- or send preceding block behind it by left shifts cost A per each? No.

Equivalent remaining-order model:
Take permutation values. Remove 1,2,...,N in order.
When removing current minimum from remaining sequence:
- if it is at front, no cost.
- otherwise either:
   * right shift prefix ending at it => cost B, then remove front
   * left shift whole prefix starting at front repeatedly until it reaches front: this takes (pos-1) left shifts, cost (pos-1)A
So cost to remove current minimum at remaining position pos is min(B, (pos-1)A).

This is optimal independently at each step because operation only affects remaining sequence by rotating prefix, and after removing minimum, relative order of remaining elements is unchanged in both methods.

Thus answer = sum over i=1..N of min(B, (pos_i_in_remaining-1)*A).

Need efficient computation of pos in remaining as we delete values in increasing order.
If val x is originally at index idx[x], then its current position among remaining elements equals number of not-yet-deleted indices <= idx[x].
Use Fenwick tree initialized with 1s.

Complexity O(N log N).
*/

public class Main {
    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in = System.in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        long nextLong() throws IOException {
            int c;
            do c = read(); while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);
            long sign = 1;
            if (c == '-') {
                sign = -1;
                c = read();
            }
            long val = 0;
            while (c > ' ') {
                val = val * 10 + (c - '0');
                c = read();
            }
            return val * sign;
        }

        int nextInt() throws IOException {
            return (int) nextLong();
        }
    }

    static class Fenwick {
        int n;
        long[] bit;
        Fenwick(int n) {
            this.n = n;
            bit = new long[n + 1];
        }
        void add(int idx, long delta) {
            for (; idx <= n; idx += idx & -idx) bit[idx] += delta;
        }
        long sum(int idx) {
            long res = 0;
            for (; idx > 0; idx -= idx & -idx) res += bit[idx];
            return res;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner();

        int N = fs.nextInt();
        long A = fs.nextLong();
        long B = fs.nextLong();

        int[] pos = new int[N + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
            int v = fs.nextInt();
            pos[v] = i;
        }

        Fenwick fw = new Fenwick(N);
        for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) fw.add(i, 1);

        long ans = 0;
        for (int v = 1; v <= N; v++) {
            long curPos = fw.sum(pos[v]); // position among remaining
            ans += Math.min(B, (curPos - 1) * A);
            fw.add(pos[v], -1);
        }

        System.out.println(ans);
    }
}
